    "section_summary": {
      "type": "string",
      "extract_value": "This Introduction reviews literature linking social safeness, emotion regulation, and body/eating psychopathology and describes body appreciation as a protective/emotion-regulation-related construct. It states the primary aim to test a mediator model in which feelings of social connectedness explain lower disordered eating via body appreciation and less rigid eating rules; no individual empirical study (e.g., 'Study 1') or sample details are reported in this section.",
      "evidence_source": "\"It is widely accepted that social safeness, i.e., feelings of social acceptance and connectedness, has its roots on early rearing interactions...\" and \"Therefore, the primary aim of the current study is to test a mediator model in which it is hypothesized that feelings of social safeness and connectedness are negatively linked to disordered eating attitudes and behaviours, through the mechanisms of different emotion regulation processes.\"",
